  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nShowers and storms will probably be ongoing Friday morning for \nportions of NE OK and NW AR. Isolated to scattered redevelopment \nwill be possible across much of the forecast area tomorrow \nafternoon and evening, with a preference for SE OK and W AR. \nAgain, a few strong to marginally severe wind gusts will be \npossible Friday afternoon and evening. Low shower and thunderstorm\nchances may linger across far E OK and NW AR Friday night into \nearly Saturday as another disturbance passes by, but mainly \ninfluences areas to our east. Then, for the most part, dry \nconditions are likely this weekend and into next week as weak-\nmoderate ridging sits overhead.\n\nTemperatures Friday will be influenced significantly by \nconvective evolution tonight into the morning hours. Higher \ncoverage of clouds and precipitation may limit heating over parts\nof NE OK and NW AR Friday. But for locations unaffected by cloud \ncover, strong afternoon heating is still expected with \ntemperatures warming into the upper 90s. Thus, heat indices \ngreater than 105 degrees will again be possible for parts of E OK \nand the AR River Valley, but confidence in exactly where this \noccurs is lower than previous days. For now, have issued a Heat \nAdvisory for portions of E OK south of Tulsa, as well as in the AR\nRiver Valley, where clouds may not be as expansive. However, \nchanges to the configuration may be required in future forecast \nupdates based on convective trends. Additional heat headlines \nthen appear probable this weekend and into early next week as high\ntemperatures remain within a few degrees of 100 and humidity \nremains elevated.",
